<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Gaetuli</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Berber pastoral peoples of the Saharan fringe south of Numidia and Mauretania, feared by Rome as raiders and prized as cavalry.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Gaetuli are known from Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, who used the name for the nomadic and semi-nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples living along the Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a>, from southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. They appear in the record from the 2nd century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They had no single government but lived in tribes under chiefs, herding horses, sheep and goats and moving with the seasons. They served as cavalry for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, and some received Roman citizenship. Gaetulian purple dye from the Atlantic coast was prized in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. They paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 6 CE they rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> and were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossus-cornelius-lentulus">Cossus Cornelius Lentulus</a>, who took the title Gaetulicus. In later centuries the name faded, and the peoples it described merged with other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> groups of the desert edge.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.72%;width:9.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Berber religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber (Libyco-Berber)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gaetulians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=gaetuli" aria-label="Gaetuli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4754 1173 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gaetuli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4830 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5074 1429h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5074 1429h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Algeria. <a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=gaetuli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

